; `a = 0 - g(1) and b`: the right side of `=` ends at `and`. The old collapse
; looks for that boundary at every second position from the first operator
; of the right side, and a call's tuple puts the operators after it on the
; other positions -- it reads `a = ((0 - g(1)) and b)`, a number as a branch
; condition: a broken module under the JIT, and compiled to a program it
; answers F1 at rc 0. The operation driver folds each
; term before the table sees the operators, so every operator stands where
; the search looks (2026-10-04); the default since 2026-10-18.
; Expected: PASS
function g(n: int) returns int
    n

function probe(a: int, b: bool) returns bool
    a = 0 - g(1) and b

if probe(0 - 1, true) and probe(5, true) = false
    print "PASS"
else
    print "FAIL"
